<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Didgori</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1121">1121</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Didgori was fought on 12 August 1121 west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">King David</a> IV the Builder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> defeated a large Seljuk-led Muslim coalition. The victory allowed David to capture Tbilisi in 1122, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Tbilisi and making the city his capital. It opened Georgia's golden age of expansion in the twelfth century.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>On the complaints of the Muslim merchants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, Ganja and Dmanisi, Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-ii-seljuk-sultan">Mahmud II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> sent an expedition into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilghazi">Ilghazi</a> ibn Artuq of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mardin">Mardin</a>. Ilghazi had pillaged the County of Edessa and defeated Roger of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> at the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ager-sanguinis">Ager Sanguinis</a> in 1119. He joined a Muslim coalition that included Tughan-Arslan, the Mazyadid Dubays ibn Sadaqa and Toghrul. In the first half of 1121 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> twice attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Ganja, so that the ruler of Arran could not take part.</p><p>The size of the coalition is debated. Walter the Chancellor and Matthew of Edessa give 600,000 and 560,000, while modern Georgian historians estimate 100,000 to 250,000, and the higher numbers are held to be exaggerated. The Georgian army numbered 56,000, including 500 Alans, about 200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and 15,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchaks</a>. King David IV had formed the mona-spa, a royal servant host of about 5,000 heavy horsemen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Muslim army entered Trialeti and encamped near Didgori and Manglisi on 10 August 1121. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> led his army from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta">Mtskheta</a> along the Nichbisi valley on 11 August and divided it, keeping a smaller group under his son Demetrius hidden behind the heights. The battle was fought on 12 August. According to Ibn al-Athir, a Kipchak detachment feigned desertion and attacked the Seljuk commanders at a meeting. David then sent his Frankish knights against the vanguard, and the cavalry charged downhill on both flanks.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilghazi">Ilghazi</a> was injured and withdrew, leaving the army leaderless. The battle was decided in about three hours, and a Georgian chronicler says the pursuit lasted three days. Matthew of Edessa writes that the corpses filled the rivers and covered the valleys and cliffs. Fugitives were hunted down for several days so that they could not regroup.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The victory allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a> to liberate the region from Muslim influence and to contest territory within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire</a>. David captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a> in 1122 and soon moved the capital there from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutaisi">Kutaisi</a>. The Georgian Chronicles celebrate Didgori as a miraculous victory, and it began the medieval Georgian Golden Age. Georgians still mark 12 August as the festival of Didgoroba.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Didgori” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 12 August 1121, at the <b>Battle of Didgori</b>, his army routed a large Muslim coalition led by the Artuqid ruler Ilghazi, and in 1122 he captured Tbilisi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1122 David IV of the Kingdom of Georgia captured Tbilisi after the <b>Battle of Didgori</b> and made it his capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-tbilisi">Emirate of Tbilisi</a></li></ul></section>
